Data that had undergone quality control section were used for association analyses in each cohort. Linear, logistic and ordinal regression models were chosen as robust and interpretable approaches to identify protein associations with AD endophenotypes. Linear regression was used to quantify the strength and direction of protein associations with continuous traits; logistic regression was used to model binary traits; and ordinal regression was applied to ordinal traits. The statsmodels (version 0.14.0) library64 in Python was used to fit the regression models and evaluate the associations. To satisfy the independence assumption required for linear, logistic and ordinal regression, only one measurement per participant was included. If a participant had two measurements, the measurement was randomly selected from either the first or second visit. For traits with a substantial number of zero values in ROSMAP (diffuse and neuritic plaques and a composite summary plaque trait to capture both types of plaques), zero values were assumed to represent levels below the detection limit. Given high correlation between the global pathology (gpath) measure in ROSMAP, which is a continuous trait with only two zero values in our dataset, and the three plaque traits mentioned above (Pearson correlations \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
