				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\ge$$\end{document}≥0.84), linear regression was applied using gpath as the dependent variable to handle these zeros through imputation. This approach enabled estimation of likely values for zero entries based on observed data patterns, preserving associations without arbitrary replacements that could skew results. Specifically, a linear regression was fitted on individuals with non-zero values, and for those with zeros, predicted values were calculated and then shrunk (divided by 10) to remain small and close to zero, maintaining alignment with the underlying data structure. To ensure model validity, residual distribution for normality was examined using quantile–quantile (Q–Q) plots, confirming that linear regression assumptions held. To improve trait normality and enhance the linear regression fit, either an inverse normal65 or Box-Cox transformation was applied to each trait. The inverse normal transformation was initially applied to all traits as it effectively adjusts for skewed distributions by replacing original values with their normal quantiles. For traits where the inverse normal transformation did not provide an optimal fit, as indicated in Q–Q plots of residuals, Box-Cox transformation was applied. This power transformation adjusts for skewness and stabilizes variance by selecting an optimal transformation parameter, which is especially effective for positive values. After transformation, outliers that fell outside the range of the mean ±3 s.d. for all traits were removed. Across all traits, the number of outliers identified was fewer than eight samples. Then, a linear regression model, as shown in equation (1), was fitted using n samples to identify significant confounding variables (age, sex and race) for each trait in each cohort:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
